ACTUAL WIDE THUMB MULTIPLE-WORD OPERATIONS IN LONG ENGLISH.

This document begins with actual expected machine encodings rather than
with a high-level algorithm. Source revision and fixture identity are
recorded explicitly. The later numerical register and memory examples are
HYPOTHETICAL INPUT STATES, not observed machine execution.

Exact external source:
  Android Open Source Project, ART,
  08490b84048a0267694268185441b70cfa090185,
  compiler/utils/assembler_thumb_test_expected.cc.inc.
  https://android.googlesource.com/platform/art/+/08490b84048a0267694268185441b70cfa090185/compiler/utils/assembler_thumb_test_expected.cc.inc

The source includes the separate arrays:
    StoreMultipleResults, and
    LoadMultipleResults.

They contain the following checked-in expected instructions:

  StoreMultipleResults, offset 0x0002:
    first halfword 0xE8A4; second halfword 0x4800;
    assembler displays: STMIA.W R4!, {R11, LR}.

  StoreMultipleResults, offset 0x0006:
    first halfword 0xE884; second halfword 0x4800;
    assembler displays: STMIA.W R4, {R11, LR}.

  LoadMultipleResults, offset 0x0002:
    first halfword 0xE934; second halfword 0x4800;
    assembler displays: LDMDB R4!, {R11, LR}.

  LoadMultipleResults, offset 0x0006:
    first halfword 0xE914; second halfword 0x4800;
    assembler displays: LDMDB R4, {R11, LR}.

The four source offsets are local to their corresponding
assembler-test arrays. It is NOT claimed that all four
instructions were consecutive in one executable program.
The original test also has other machine instructions,
and this document does not pretend to translate all
instructions reachable from its true entry point.

PART ONE. FIRST FETCH, THEN SECOND HALFWORD OF THE SAME INSTRUCTION.

At each of these four offsets, the first five bits of
the first halfword begin with binary 11101, identifying
a 32-bit T32 encoding class. A second 16-bit halfword
must be fetched and interpreted as part of the same
architectural instruction.

The second halfword 0x4800 has bits 14 and 11 equal
to one. These select register number 14 (LR) and
register number 11 (R11), respectively. Bit 15
(selecting PC where such a field is defined) is
zero. Bit 13 (SP) is zero. Both registers are
included in each transfer.

The exact first halfword distinguishes four
architectural operations:
  0xE884: increment-after multiword STORE, W=0;
  0xE8A4: increment-after multiword STORE, W=1;
  0xE914: decrement-before multiword LOAD, W=0;
  0xE934: decrement-before multiword LOAD, W=1.

The bit which differs between 0xE884 and 0xE8A4
is the writeback bit. The analogous difference
between 0xE914 and 0xE934 is also writeback.
Changing that bit can change R4 even when
the same data values are transferred.

These are FOUR-BYTE instructions. An instruction
at offset 0x0002 occupies halfwords beginning at
0x0002 and 0x0004, and has ordinary successor
index 0x0006. An instruction at offset 0x0006
occupies halfwords 0x0006 and 0x0008 and has
ordinary successor index 0x000A.
The second halfword of a four-byte instruction
is NOT the next independently executed instruction.

PART TWO. STORE MULTIPLE WITH WRITEBACK.

Assume the instruction at StoreMultipleResults
offset 0x0002 is the current instruction. Define
the starting state as:
    R4 = 0x00003000;
    R11 = 0x11223344;
    LR = 0x00004001;
    N, Z, C, V are unknown but fixed;
    memory at 0x3000..0x3007 is mapped,
    writable, and satisfies alignment rules;
    no exception occurs during the transfer.

The selected operation 62 has the long name:
"Beginning at the memory address in a selected
general-purpose base register, copy the original
thirty-two-bit value of every member of a permitted
register set into consecutive four-byte memory
words ordered by ascending register number,
and then increase the base register by four bytes
per member."

There are two selected registers, numbered 11 and 14.
The lower-numbered one is R11, so the memory word
at address 0x3000 receives the original R11 value
0x11223344. The higher-numbered one is LR,
so the word at address 0x3004 receives 0x00004001.

Since two registers were selected, R4 changes from
0x3000 to 0x3008. R11 remains 0x11223344;
LR remains 0x00004001; flags remain fixed.
No branch is caused by storing LR as data.
The successor execution index becomes offset 0x0006
in the same StoreMultipleResults fragment.

PART THREE. STORE MULTIPLE WITHOUT WRITEBACK.

For the different instruction at StoreMultipleResults
offset 0x0006, reset the starting state to
R4=0x3000, R11=0x11223344, LR=0x00004001
and valid mapped/writable aligned memory.

This instruction is operation 61, whose long name
states that the base register shall remain unchanged.

The word at 0x3000 becomes 0x11223344.
The word at 0x3004 becomes 0x00004001.
R4 REMAINS 0x3000.
The flags, R11 and LR remain unchanged.
The successor index is offset 0x000A.

The memory effects match the writeback case
under equal starting states, but the complete
machine-state effects do not. The difference
must survive translation to English.

PART FOUR. LOAD MULTIPLE, DECREMENT BEFORE, WRITEBACK.

Begin a SEPARATE model input at LoadMultipleResults
offset 0x0002. Assume:
    R4=0x00003008;
    memory word at 0x3000 = 0x11223344;
    memory word at 0x3004 = 0x00004001;
    those words are readable and aligned.

Instruction halfwords 0xE934,0x4800 invoke
operation 64. Two registers are selected.
The FIRST word address is:
    0x3008 minus 4 times 2 = 0x3000.

The lowest selected register R11 receives
the word from 0x3000, so R11=0x11223344.

The highest selected register LR receives
the word from 0x3004, so LR=0x00004001.

The writeback bit is set, so R4 becomes
0x3008 minus eight bytes = 0x3000.
The flags remain unchanged. NO BRANCH
occurs from the mere act of loading LR.
The next execution index is offset 0x0006
within LoadMultipleResults.

PART FIVE. LOAD MULTIPLE, DECREMENT BEFORE, NO WRITEBACK.

Begin another separate model input at LoadMultipleResults
offset 0x0006 with R4=0x00003008 and the
same two readable aligned memory words.

Instruction halfwords 0xE914,0x4800 invoke
operation 63. The load addresses and
register assignments are the SAME as above:
R11 becomes 0x11223344 and LR becomes 0x00004001.

But here R4 REMAINS 0x3008 rather than
becoming 0x3000. The execution index
advances by four bytes to offset 0x000A.
Flags remain unchanged. No PC write occurs.

PART SIX. WHY LOADING LR DOES NOT AUTOMATICALLY RETURN.

The link register is capable of holding
a Thumb-state-tagged return value, but
a data-load instruction that makes LR
equal to 0x00004001 is not a branch.
The next ordinary instruction is still
at the sequential successor address.

A later authorized BX LR or equivalent
control-transfer instruction could read
LR and transfer to 0x00004000 in T32
state, subject to that target being
permitted and executable. Such a branch
was NOT observed as part of these four
machine instructions. It must not be
inserted as an alleged translation
of LDMDB R4!,{R11,LR}.

Likewise, a store of the LR data word
0x00004001 does not execute any of
the bytes of that word.

PART SEVEN. EFFECTS NOT CLAIMED HERE.

The memory words at 0x3000 and 0x3004
are not an atomic combined eight-byte
transaction simply because one instruction
addresses both words. A failing second
access cannot be assumed to roll back
the first. A multiword instruction may
fault, and the exact partial effects
need their own architectural rule.

Registers R11 and LR remain legal members
of these selected WIDE operations, unlike
the narrow T1 STMIA/LDMIA set restricted
to R0-R7. Conversely, SP and PC are not
granted list members by these particular
long operation names. The subset's base
cannot overlap its register set.

The hypothetical instruction stream
starts at a relative offset, not at
an established Android process VA.
No claim about the actual physical
DRAM component, cache line fills,
branch prediction, process privilege,
or executed binary is derived from
the source assembler expectations.

PART EIGHT. COMPETING REPRESENTATIONS TO RETAIN.

Choice A: treat the hardware's W bit as
one source operation with a Boolean
writeback option. This is compact, but
a reader must inspect that option to
understand whether the base changes.

Choice B: give base-preserving and
base-updating operations different
long English names, as entries 61-64
currently do. This makes a crucial
state change visibly explicit, at the
cost of longer source and more names.

Choice C: replace a multiword operation
by multiple single-word loads or stores
and a separately explicit base update.
This makes individual memory addresses
easy to spell out but must account for
all exceptional effects and ordering
before claiming equivalence.

Choice D: represent the complete exact
native instruction as a foreign opaque
operation and attach an explanatory
English comment. This avoids inventing
semantics prematurely, but fails the
goal of a genuinely exhaustive English
translation unless the foreign operation
is precisely defined elsewhere.

Current draft choice: separate long
operations according to writeback,
preserve the original halfwords and
provenance, and list any unverified
processor-level effects without guessing.

PART NINE. POSITIVE AND NEGATIVE CASES.

Positive: the four halfword pairs
must be decoded as FOUR-BYTE T32
instructions with the listed base,
two-register mask, and W value.

Positive: when base=0x3000, either
STMIA stores R11 at 0x3000 and
LR at 0x3004. With writeback,
R4 becomes 0x3008. Without, R4
remains 0x3000.

Positive: when base=0x3008, either
LDMDB loads R11 from 0x3000 and
LR from 0x3004. With writeback,
R4 becomes 0x3000. Without, R4
remains 0x3008.

Negative: interpreting halfword
0x4800 as its own instruction
after 0xE8A4, 0xE884, 0xE934
or 0xE914 must be rejected.

Negative: loading LR must not
be reported as a branch or as
an update of the execution index
to the value held in LR.

Negative: decrement-before must
not be confused with increment-after.
The starting word for a two-register
LDMDB with base 0x3008 is 0x3000,
not 0x3008.

Negative: a base-register writeback
must not be invented in a W=0 form
or omitted in a W=1 form.

Negative: the register list order
must not be reversed; R11 is the
lower-numbered register despite
LR's special linguistic name.

Negative: even though the external
AOSP source includes those encodings,
the presence of assembly text alone
does not show that an English Major C
parser, translator or emulator executed
these instructions.

Every one of the cases above is a
SPECIFIED FUTURE ACCEPTANCE CASE,
not an already accepted program test.
